<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Hris.Foundation.WorkflowEngine
    Sprint 5 (Workflow Engine and Notification Framework) scope, per IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md
    Phase 1: first of Sprint 5's own two frameworks, built as a genuine mutual
    dependency cycle with Notification Framework but as its own separate PR, the same
    "one framework, one PR, even within one Sprint" discipline Sprint 3's own
    nine-framework kernel and Sprint 4's own eight frameworks already establish.
    Source specification: docs/03-foundation/workflow-engine.md, built entirely from
    scratch this Sprint: no Domain layer existed for this one before this pass.

    Deliberately narrower than the source document's own full Scope, the same
    "records the configuration, does not build the runtime that walks it" split every
    Sprint 4 config-plus-occurrence framework already draws for its own out-of-scope
    runtime concern:

    - No visual, drag-and-drop Workflow Designer. That is this document's own Presentation
      layer/frontend concern (docs/07-ui-ux/), not a backend Domain/Application/
      Infrastructure build; this framework provides the commands/queries a future
      designer UI calls, not the UI itself.
    - No condition/branch/loop evaluator that reads live business data through another
      module's public interface and decides which step runs next automatically. This
      build records a WorkflowDefinitionVersion's own ordered step sequence and exposes
      commands to advance a WorkflowInstance from one step to the next, but the actual
      orchestrator that watches for trigger events, evaluates conditions against real
      data, and calls Advance/RequestApproval/Complete unattended is a real operational
      runtime: the identical "does not build the actual worker process" scope boundary
      Job Processing Framework's own remarks already draw for itself, and Scheduling
      Framework's own "does not evaluate cron expressions."
    - No standing Delegation Rule registry (Temporary/Permanent/Out-of-Office,
      Effective Dates) that auto-applies to every future task assigned to a user:
      WorkflowTask.Delegate is a one-time, already-created-task redirect only (see that
      method's own remarks).
    - No AI-suggested workflows, natural-language authoring, simulation/dry-run testing,
      or marketplace distribution: this document's own Future Enhancements section
      states plainly these are "not required for initial delivery."
    - No actual SLA-timer background job driving Expire/Escalate automatically: this
      framework exposes those as callable commands; a real scheduler invoking them on a
      timer is Scheduling/Job Processing Frameworks' own concern, already built.

    This Sprint builds what the document's own AI Implementation Guidance actually names
    as non-negotiable: actions as invocations of public module commands rather than
    direct data manipulation, idempotent-by-design retryable actions, definition
    versioning where a running instance never changes behavior mid-flight, permanent
    workflow execution history, self-approval routing rejected at publication, and
    canonical role names for every participant.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Hris.Foundation.WorkflowEngine</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" Version="12.4.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" Version="11.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions" Version="11.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="9.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="9.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Hris.SharedKernel\Hris.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Hris.Application\Hris.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Hris.Infrastructure\Hris.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
